Keyword-valued shape attributes are written with `:assign`, not `:set`.

Penpot's JSON middleware keywordises change-operation *keys* and leaves
*values* alone, and `:set` types its `val` as `::sm/any`, so nothing coerces
`"flex"` into `:flex`. `validate-shape` then rejects the literal string
against `[::sm/one-of #{:grid :flex}]` and update-file 500s. That took out
every enum-valued attribute — all of auto-layout, blend modes, constraints,
grow type — and `set_stroke` outright, since `build_stroke` always emits
`stroke-style` and `stroke-alignment`.

`:assign` is the operation that decodes: its handler runs
`(sm/decoder cts/schema:shape-attrs sm/json-transformer)` over the value map
with the shape's own type in hand — the same coercion `add-obj` gets, which
is why creating a shape with a stroke style always worked and setting one
never did. Invalid enum members are still rejected; this adds coercion, not
a bypass.

Also: `set_layout`'s `padding` was one scalar written to all four sides.
Per-side overrides plus `layout-padding-type` make asymmetric padding
expressible; the scalar call shape is unchanged.

THE OBJECTION AGAINST THIS PATCH, which is real and unresolved.

Penpot's own `changes_builder.cljc` emits `:set` everywhere and never emits
`:assign`. The handler is schema-supported, implemented, and measured
working here — but this server is very likely its primary user, so it is far
less exercised than `:set`, and a reviewer would be right to say the case
for it rests on grepping `common/` and `backend/` and inferring about a
60k-line frontend that was not read.

One concrete cost is already known and was found by reading rather than by
testing: `components-changed` and `frames-changed` both match
`(= (:type operation) :set)`, so an `:assign` op on an attribute in
`sync-attrs` is invisible to them — no component synchronisation, no
frame-thumbnail invalidation. What else pattern-matches `:set` in the
frontend (undo/redo construction, the shape-modifier pipeline, the websocket
broadcast to other connected editors) is not known.

That is why this is a SPLIT and not a swap. Every attribute routed to
`:assign` is one that cannot be written at all under `:set`, so nothing that
works today changes operation, and the exposure is confined to attributes
whose current behaviour is a hard 500. The failure mode of a missed
pattern-match is a silently skipped side-effect, not corruption: invalid
data is still rejected and failed writes roll back without consuming a
revision.

The revisit trigger is specific: the first time a component copy needs to
inherit an enum-valued attribute from its main.

